<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="c7ea1d32">It is useful to have a standard way of defining principles. In addition to a definition statement, each principle should
have an associated rationale and implication statement for ease of understanding and acceptance, as well as support the use
of the principles to explain and justify the logic supporting specific decisions.&lt;p/&gt;&lt;p&gt;
    Principles are required to develop the architecture. Architecture principles are general rules and guidelines, intended
    to be enduring and seldom amended, that inform and support the way in which a project sets about fulfilling its
    objectives. Principles may be just one element in a structured set of ideas that collectively define and guide the
    project, from values through to actions and results. Principles are generally created for each architecture domain,
    e.g. Business principles, Data principles, Application principles, Technology principles.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="461a393a"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="673816e1"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="42f105ca"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="51cde10a">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of this artifact is to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Serve as a firm foundation to develop plans, architecture and design decisions; frame policies, procedures and
        standards; and support resolution of contradictory situations.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Serve as a guide to establish relevant evaluation criteria for selection of products and solutions.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Help manage compliance of solution architectures to the enterprise architecture.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Inform constraints that need to be tracked from business and IT perspectives.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p/&gt;&lt;ul class=&quot;noindent&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To&amp;nbsp;inform, harmonise and enable joint decision-making.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To&amp;nbsp;de-risk transformation by providing guiding rules on technology and solution choices.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To&amp;nbsp;reduce the need for re-design and retrofitting by enforcing established standards.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To&amp;nbsp;bring in structure, rigor, and discipline to architecture governance.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To&amp;nbsp;assist in reducing Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) for IT.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To help guide design decisions.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="e9153045"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="f15ea71c"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="d74501da">&lt;p&gt;
    List of elements captured for each principle:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Name&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Represents the essence of the rule and is easy to remember
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Specific technology platforms should not be mentioned in the name of a principle
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Avoid ambiguous words in the name such as &quot;support&quot;, &quot;open&quot; and &quot;consider&quot;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Statement&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Communicates, succinctly and unambiguously, the fundamental rule
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Specific technology platforms should not be mentioned in the statement of a principle
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Avoid ambiguous words in statement such as &quot;support&quot;, &quot;open&quot; and &quot;consider”
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Principle statements for managing information are typically similar from one organization to another
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Highlights the business benefits of adhering to the principles using business terminology
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Points to similarities between the information/technology principles and the principles governing business
        operations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Describes the relationship to other principles and the intentions regarding a balanced interpretation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Describes situations where one principle may be given precedence or carry more weight than another for making a
        decision
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Implications&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Highlights the requirements, both for the business and IT, to carry out the principle in terms of resources, costs,
        activities and tasks
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Current systems, standards or practices are often apparently incongruent with the principle upon adoption
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Impact to the business and consequences of adopting a principle should be clearly stated
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The reader should readily discern the answer to &quot;How does this affect me?&quot;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Avoid oversimplifying, trivializing or judging the merit of the impact
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Some implications will be identified as potential impacts only and may be speculative rather than fully analyzed
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
